<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kapisa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kapisi, Begram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kapisa was an ancient city and region north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, at modern Begram near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagram">Bagram</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. It was a summer capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>, and a hoard of Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a>, Roman glass and Chinese lacquer found there shows its place in long-distance trade. The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> described it in 630 as a prosperous kingdom with many Buddhist monasteries, and it was later a seat of the Nezak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and the Turk Shahis.</p></div>
